  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>A sublet can be the best housing deal you ever find: a furnished place, a flexible end date, and no twelve-month commitment. It can also turn into a mess if you and the person leaving never talked through the basics. Most sublet problems are not dramatic scams. They are small misunderstandings that grow: nobody knew who paid the internet bill, the building manager had never heard of you, or the deposit came back three months late with no explanation.</p>
<p>The fix is simple. Ask the right questions before you sign or send money, and get the answers in writing. Here are the questions worth asking, grouped by topic, with notes on what a good answer sounds like.</p>

<h2>Permission and paperwork</h2>

<h3>1. Does your landlord know about this sublet, and have they approved it?</h3>
<p>This is the most important question on the list. Many leases require the tenant to get written permission before subletting, and some prohibit it outright. If the landlord has not approved the arrangement, you could be asked to leave with little warning, and you would have no direct relationship with the owner to fall back on.</p>
<p>A good answer: "Yes, here is the email (or signed form) from the property manager." A worrying answer: "It's fine, they never check." If you want background on how this works from the host's side, our guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-sublet-your-apartment-legally/">subletting an apartment legally</a> covers it in detail.</p>

<h3>2. Will we sign a written sublease agreement?</h3>
<p>Even a friendly, informal arrangement deserves a written agreement. It should name both parties, the address, the dates, the rent, the deposit, what is included, and what happens if either person needs to end early. See <a href="/blog/sublease-agreement-what-to-include/">what to include in a sublease agreement</a> for a full checklist.</p>

<h3>3. Can I see the relevant part of the original lease?</h3>
<p>As a subtenant, you are usually expected to follow the rules of the main lease: quiet hours, pet rules, smoking rules, guest limits. You cannot follow rules you have never seen. Ask for the sections that apply to you, at minimum.</p>

<h2>Money</h2>

<h3>4. Who do I pay, how, and when?</h3>
<p>Clarify whether you pay the tenant or the landlord directly, the due date each month, and the payment method. Prefer methods that leave a record, like a bank transfer or a check, and keep receipts. Be very cautious with wire transfers, gift cards, or cryptocurrency, especially before you have seen the place. Those are the payment methods scammers prefer because they are hard to reverse. Our article on <a href="/blog/how-to-avoid-rental-scams/">how to avoid rental scams</a> goes deeper.</p>

<h3>5. What happens with partial months?</h3>
<p>If you move in on the 12th, do you pay a prorated amount for the first month? Agree on the math ahead of time. Our <a href="/tools/proration/">proration calculator</a> makes this easy to settle in a minute.</p>

<h3>6. How much is the deposit, and when and how will it come back?</h3>
<p>Ask the amount, who holds it (the tenant or the landlord), what it can be used for, and the timeline for returning it after you move out. Agree on how you will both document the condition of the place at move-in. We cover this in more depth in <a href="/blog/security-deposits-for-sublets/">security deposits for sublets</a>.</p>

<h2>What's included</h2>

<h3>7. Which utilities are included in the rent, and which do I pay separately?</h3>
<p>Electricity, gas, water, trash, internet, and streaming accounts all need an owner. Common setups include "everything included at a flat rate" or "rent plus a split of the actual bills." If you will split bills, ask what a typical month costs, especially in summer or winter when heating and cooling can swing the total. If you are budgeting across several options, the <a href="/tools/cost-calculator/">cost calculator</a> helps you compare the real monthly number.</p>

<h3>8. What exactly comes with the furniture?</h3>
<p>"Furnished" can mean a bed and a couch, or it can mean a fully stocked kitchen with linens and a hair dryer. Ask specifically: Is there bedding? Towels? Pots, pans, and dishes? A desk and chair? A TV? Will any of the host's belongings be stored in closets or drawers, and how much space will be free for you? Knowing this tells you what to bring; our guide on <a href="/blog/what-to-pack-for-a-sublet/">what to pack for a sublet</a> can fill in the rest.</p>

<h3>9. Are there shared spaces or roommates?</h3>
<p>If roommates are staying, ask to meet them, even by video. Find out how shared spaces, chores, and groceries work. A five-minute conversation can save you a summer of awkwardness.</p>

<h3>10. What about laundry, parking, storage, and building amenities?</h3>
<p>Ask whether laundry is in-unit, in the building, or down the street, and whether it needs a card or app. If you have a car, confirm whether parking is included or if you would need a permit. If the building has a gym or package room, confirm you will have access.</p>

<h2>Logistics</h2>

<h3>11. How will I get the keys, fobs, and access codes?</h3>
<p>Settle the handoff in advance: in person, through a neighbor, a lockbox, or the front desk. Ask how many keys there are, whether you will get a building fob or garage remote, and whether the building needs your name on file to let you in. Agree on how the keys get returned at the end.</p>

<h3>12. How should I handle mail and packages?</h3>
<p>Should your mail come to this address at all? If so, does your name need to be added to the mailbox or the building's package system? Also ask what the host wants you to do with their mail: forward it, hold it, or leave it.</p>

<h3>13. Who do I contact when something breaks?</h3>
<p>Find out who handles repairs. Do you report problems to the host, who then contacts the landlord, or can you contact maintenance directly? Get phone numbers for both, plus any emergency line. Ask who pays if something you did not cause stops working.</p>

<h2>Changes of plan</h2>

<h3>14. What happens if either of us needs to end the sublet early?</h3>
<p>Plans change. Internships get extended, travel nurse contracts get cancelled, and hosts come home early. Agree on a notice period and what happens to rent and the deposit if someone leaves before the end date. Put it in the agreement.</p>

<h3>15. Is there any chance of an extension?</h3>
<p>If you think you might want to stay longer, say so up front. The host may know whether their lease, landlord, or own plans allow it. It is far easier to plan for an extension than to scramble for a new place with two weeks' notice.</p>

<h2>How to ask without sounding suspicious</h2>
<p>Some renters worry that a long list of questions will make them look difficult. In practice, good hosts appreciate it. A subtenant who asks careful questions is usually one who will take care of the apartment and pay on time. Frame it as getting on the same page: "I want to make sure we're both clear on everything, so here are a few things I'd like to confirm."</p>
<p>Ask by message or email rather than only on the phone, so you have the answers in writing. Then make sure the important points end up in your sublease agreement.</p>

<h2>Red flags to watch for</h2>
<ul>
<li>The host will not show you the place, in person or on a live video call. See our guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-tour-a-sublet-remotely/">touring a sublet remotely</a>.</li>
<li>They want a deposit or rent before you have seen the place or confirmed they have the right to rent it.</li>
<li>They push you to pay by wire, gift card, or crypto.</li>
<li>They dodge the landlord question or get irritated when you ask it.</li>
<li>The price is far below every comparable listing in the area, with urgency attached.</li>
</ul>
<p>Any one of these is a reason to slow down. Several together are a reason to walk away. Our <a href="/safety/">safety page</a> has more on spotting trouble early.</p>

<h2>Ready to start looking?</h2>
<p>When you browse <a href="/sublets/">sublets on The Sublet</a>, you can message hosts through our relay contact form, so your email address is not exposed until you choose to share it. Keep this list handy, copy the questions that apply, and send them along with your first message. A host who answers clearly and promptly is a good sign of a smooth stay.</p>
